From 18c3de30e5886cfd8f7c79257f28601748732555 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 19:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 72/74] nouveau/vk: an allocation may say it need not start out
 zero
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 7 item 1 (CPU cost of submission)
Why: on the Horizon backend every nvkmd allocation comes out of this
 process's own heap through nvmap, and horizon_gpu_mem_create memsets it
 to zero before returning. Nothing in nvkmd could say it did not need
 that, so every allocation paid it — including the ones NVK itself
 declares it is not relying on, and including the ones NVK then fills
 again for VK_MEMORY_ALLOCATE_ZERO_INITIALIZE_BIT_EXT.
Evidence: measured on a console 2026-09-07, gpu_memory/alloc, the fill
 alone: 21 us of a 64 KiB allocation, 182 us of a 1 MiB one and 1403 us
 of an 8 MiB one — 1992 us against 589 us for the same 8 MiB with
 horizon_gpu_mem_create_uninit. Recorded in
 docs/MEASURED-ON-HARDWARE.md, "What zeroing an allocation costs".
Upstream: no — the flag is meaningful only on a backend that fills in
 user space, and this is the only one.

THE FLAG IS A HINT A BACKEND MAY IGNORE AND A PROMISE THE CALLER MAY
NOT. NVKMD_MEM_NO_ZERO_INIT asserts that every byte anything will ever
read from the object is written first. The nouveau backend ignores it
and must: its pages come from the kernel, which zeroes them because
they may have belonged to ANOTHER process, and that is a guarantee no
caller of this interface is in a position to waive. It also has nothing
to save, since that backend never fills them itself. Both halves are
written down where each backend allocates.

ONE CONSUMER TAKES IT, AND THE AUDIT IS WHY THE OTHERS DO NOT.

vkAllocateMemory (nvk_device_memory.c). Vulkan says nothing about the
contents of a VkDeviceMemory; an application that reads a byte before
writing one has undefined contents on every implementation. The one
case where zeroes are promised —
VK_MEMORY_ALLOCATE_ZERO_INITIALIZE_BIT_EXT — this file already honours
itself, with a memset or an upload-queue fill that it synchronises, as
it does for NVK_DEBUG=zero_memory and NVK_DEBUG=trash_memory.
`enum nvk_memory_init` is that decision, and NVK_MEMORY_INIT_NONE is
NVK saying it is not relying on the contents. So on a backend that
zeroes every allocation, the ZERO case is filled TWICE and the NONE
case is filled for nobody. Nothing crosses a process boundary: the
memory is this process's own, and the nouveau backend ignores the flag
precisely so that it keeps mattering there. Imports are excluded
because they allocate nothing.

COMMAND-BUFFER CHUNKS TOOK IT AND A CONSOLE TOOK IT BACK. The first
version of this patch flagged nvk_cmd_pool.c too, on the argument that
the GPU is only ever pointed at [addr, addr + used) and nv_push writes
every dword up to `used`. The same pool also backs
nvk_cmd_buffer_upload_alloc, whose bytes the GPU reads as DATA, and
whose new-chunk path never recorded `used_B` — so flush_mem_list could
skip the clean for a chunk the CPU had written, and what the GPU then
read was whatever the fill used to put there. MEASURED 2026-09-07,
vk_core/transfer section C: "48/64 words match — first mismatch at word
0: got 0xff97803c, want 0xa5dac00d (16 wrong)", one cache line of a
vkCmdUpdateBuffer arriving as heap rubbish, on a case that had passed
for weeks. The missing used_B is the next patch; the fill stays on
these chunks regardless, because the rule the flag was introduced under
is that a consumer takes it only where every observable byte is SHOWN
to be written first, and this one has now been shown not to be.

WHAT ELSE KEEPS THE FILL, and why:

  - descriptor pools and the descriptor tables in nvk_mem_arena /
    nvk_heap. A descriptor the application never wrote is read by
    HARDWARE, at whatever index a shader forms, and the failure would
    be a wrong texture rather than an error.
  - query pools. vkGetQueryPoolResults reads an availability word and
    the reset that writes it is the application's to issue; garbage
    there is a result reported as ready.
  - nvk_mem_stream's chunks, for the reason the command pool lost the
    flag: they are handed out as data and recycled.
  - nvk_device.c's zero page, the QMD heap, the shader heap and
    everything else NVK reads back or hands to a fixed-function unit.
  - the hang recorder in horizon_gpu, which is not reached from here
    and keeps the fill for its own reason: the zeros are how "the GPU
    never reached this slot" is recognised.

NVK_HORIZON_ZERO_ALL_MEM=1 turns the skip off again for allocations
that asked for it, and the backend says once in the log when it has
actually skipped one. Homebrew is launched with no environment, so the
only way to compare the two shapes at one clock is a case setting the
variable on itself between two runs — the same shape as
MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT and NVK_HORIZON_BATCH_EXEC — and
the log line is what tells a run that measured no difference whether
the flag arrived at all. It is also the first thing to reach for if an
application misbehaves after this patch.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01YajZtX9CeYzC35x2t1QJJh
---
 src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_pool.c             | 21 +++++++
 src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c        | 32 +++++++++-
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h      | 11 ++++
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_mem.c  | 60 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/nouveau/nvkmd_nouveau_mem.c  |  8 +++
 src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nvkmd.h              | 30 ++++++++++
 6 files changed, 158 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_pool.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_pool.c
index fbabd31..2dd3796 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_pool.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_pool.c
@@ -21,6 +21,27 @@ nvk_cmd_mem_create(struct nvk_cmd_pool *pool, bool force_gart, struct nvk_cmd_me
    if (mem == NULL)
       return vk_error(pool, VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY);
 
+   /* NO NVKMD_MEM_NO_ZERO_INIT HERE, AND A CONSOLE IS WHY.
+    *
+    * The audit that added the flag said a chunk is only ever a
+    * pushbuffer — the GPU pointed at [addr, addr + used), every dword
+    * up to `used` written by nv_push. That is not the whole truth: the
+    * same pool also backs nvk_cmd_buffer_upload_alloc, whose bytes the
+    * GPU reads as DATA, and its new-chunk path does not record
+    * `used_B`, so flush_mem_list can skip the clean for a chunk the CPU
+    * has written. With the allocator filling, the bytes the GPU then
+    * read were zeros; without it they are whatever the heap held.
+    *
+    * MEASURED ON A CONSOLE 2026-09-07: vk_core/transfer, section C,
+    * "C inline update landed: 48/64 words match — first mismatch at
+    * word 0: got 0xff97803c, want 0xa5dac00d (16 wrong)". Sixty-four
+    * bytes of a vkCmdUpdateBuffer, one cache line, arriving as heap
+    * rubbish. 202 of 203 checks, on a case that had passed for weeks.
+    *
+    * The missing used_B is fixed in its own patch. The fill stays off
+    * this allocation anyway: the rule the flag was introduced under is
+    * that a consumer takes it only where every observable byte is shown
+    * to be written first, and this one has now been shown not to be. */
    const uint32_t flags = force_gart ? NVKMD_MEM_GART
                                      : NVKMD_MEM_LOCAL;
 
diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c
index e502936..ba5b2a4 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c
@@ -202,9 +202,39 @@ nvk_AllocateMemory(VkDevice device,
       }
    }
 
-   const enum nvkmd_mem_flags flags =
+   enum nvkmd_mem_flags flags =
       nvk_memory_type_flags(type, handle_types, pinned_to_vram);
 
+   /* THE ALLOCATOR NEED NOT ZERO THIS ONE, and this function is the
+    * proof rather than the assumption.
+    *
+    * Vulkan says nothing about the contents of vkAllocateMemory: an
+    * application that reads a byte of it before writing one has
+    * undefined contents by the specification, on every implementation.
+    * The one case where zeroes are promised is
+    * VK_MEMORY_ALLOCATE_ZERO_INITIALIZE_BIT_EXT, and this file already
+    * honours that itself, below, with a memset or an upload-queue fill
+    * that it then synchronises — as it does for NVK_DEBUG=zero_memory
+    * and NVK_DEBUG=trash_memory. `enum nvk_memory_init` is that
+    * decision, and NVK_MEMORY_INIT_NONE is NVK saying it is not
+    * relying on the contents.
+    *
+    * So on a backend that zeroes every allocation, the ZERO case is
+    * filled TWICE and the NONE case is filled for nobody. On the
+    * Horizon backend that fill is a memset this process pays for out of
+    * its own heap — 1403 us of an 8 MiB allocation, measured on a
+    * console on 2026-09-07 — and it is the largest single cost in
+    * vkAllocateMemory there.
+    *
+    * NOTHING CROSSES A PROCESS BOUNDARY. The concern the flag's comment
+    * in nvkmd.h raises about the kernel's zeroing does not arise here:
+    * the memory is this process's own, and the nouveau backend ignores
+    * the flag precisely so that it keeps arising there.
+    *
+    * The import path is excluded because it allocates nothing. */
+   if (!(fd_info && fd_info->handleType))
+      flags |= NVKMD_MEM_NO_ZERO_INIT;
+
    const uint64_t aligned_size =
       align64(pAllocateInfo->allocationSize, alignment);
 
diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h
index a1b104c..a82c830 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h
@@ -97,6 +97,17 @@ struct nvkmd_horizon_dev {
    /* Borrowed from the pdev; not owned. See the header comment. */
    horizon_gpu_device *gpu;
 
+   /* Whether this device has already said that it skipped a zero fill.
+    *
+    * PER DEVICE AND NOT PER PROCESS, because a test is what reads it
+    * and a test's log scan sees only what its own case produced: one
+    * .nro holds several cases, each of which builds its own device, and
+    * a process-wide flag would put the line in whichever case happened
+    * to allocate first. Not synchronised — the worst a race can do is
+    * print the line twice, and a mutex to make a log line rarer is a
+    * mutex for nothing. */
+   bool said_no_zero_fill;
+
    /* Every live VA of this device, so a GPU address can be named.
     *
     * An MMU fault is the one GPU failure that says nothing useful on its
diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_mem.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_mem.c
index 0d191a4..b30db0c 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_mem.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_mem.c
@@ -262,14 +262,68 @@ nvkmd_horizon_alloc_mem_kind(struct nvkmd_dev *_dev,
       (flags & NVKMD_MEM_COHERENT) ? HORIZON_GPU_MEM_UNCACHED
                                    : HORIZON_GPU_MEM_CACHED;
 
+   /* THE ZERO FILL, AND WHO IS ALLOWED TO SKIP IT.
+    *
+    * horizon_gpu_mem_create zeroes; horizon_gpu_mem_create_uninit does
+    * not, and is otherwise the same function — same rounding, same
+    * nvmap registration, same cache maintenance, and in particular the
+    * same unconditional armDCacheFlush, which is there for the dirty
+    * lines the previous tenant of this heap memory left behind and not
+    * as the fill's cleanup. Skipping the fill is therefore a memset
+    * this process does not do, and nothing else.
+    *
+    * Only for callers that passed NVKMD_MEM_NO_ZERO_INIT. There is
+    * deliberately no environment variable that turns it on for
+    * allocations that did not ask.
+    *
+    * NVK_HORIZON_ZERO_ALL_MEM=1 turns it OFF for allocations that did,
+    * which is the opposite direction and is the only way to measure
+    * what it buys: homebrew is launched with no environment, so an A/B
+    * is a case setting this on itself between two runs at one clock —
+    * the same shape as MESA_VK_WSI_HORIZON_CPU_ACQUIRE_WAIT and
+    * NVK_HORIZON_BATCH_EXEC. It is also the first thing to try when an
+    * application misbehaves after this patch: if zeroing everything
+    * fixes it, the application is reading memory it never wrote and the
+    * consumer that asked for the flag is wrong.
+    *
+    * READ PER ALLOCATION, AND ONLY WHEN IT COULD MATTER. Caching it in
+    * a static would make the variable a property of the process, and
+    * then the A/B it exists for could not happen: a case has to set it
+    * on itself between two runs at one clock, because a .nro is
+    * launched with no environment at all. The getenv behind
+    * debug_get_bool_option is a walk of an environment homebrew keeps
+    * small, next to an nvmap registration and a page-aligned
+    * allocation — and it is not reached at all unless the caller asked
+    * to skip the fill. */
+   const bool skip_fill =
+      (flags & NVKMD_MEM_NO_ZERO_INIT) &&
+      !debug_get_bool_option("NVK_HORIZON_ZERO_ALL_MEM", false);
+
+   /* SAID ONCE PER DEVICE, so a test can prove the flag arrived.
+    * Without it there is no way to tell "the flag reached the allocator
+    * and the saving is inside the noise" from "the flag never reached
+    * the allocator" — and on 2026-09-07 vk_core/device_memory measured
+    * a difference of zero and could not say which it had. Per device
+    * rather than per process because that is the scope a case's log
+    * scan can see; see struct nvkmd_horizon_dev. */
+   if (skip_fill && !dev->said_no_zero_fill) {
+      dev->said_no_zero_fill = true;
+      mesa_logi("nvkmd_horizon: an allocation asked not to be zero filled "
+                "and was not (%" PRIu64 " bytes); "
+                "NVK_HORIZON_ZERO_ALL_MEM=1 turns this off", size_B);
+   }
+
    horizon_gpu_result res =
-      horizon_gpu_mem_create(dev->gpu, size_B, align, policy,
-                             &mem->gpu_mem);
+      skip_fill ? horizon_gpu_mem_create_uninit(dev->gpu, size_B, align,
+                                                policy, &mem->gpu_mem)
+                : horizon_gpu_mem_create(dev->gpu, size_B, align, policy,
+                                         &mem->gpu_mem);
    if (res.status != HORIZON_GPU_OK) {
       FREE(mem);
       return vk_errorf(log_obj, nvkmd_horizon_result(res),
-                       "horizon_gpu_mem_create(%" PRIu64 ", %" PRIu64 ") "
+                       "horizon_gpu_mem_create%s(%" PRIu64 ", %" PRIu64 ") "
                        "failed: status %d, nv 0x%08" PRIx32,
+                       skip_fill ? "_uninit" : "",
                        size_B, align, (int)res.status, res.nv);
    }
 
diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nouveau/nvkmd_nouveau_mem.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nouveau/nvkmd_nouveau_mem.c
index 6bd7d7c..127fa67 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nouveau/nvkmd_nouveau_mem.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nouveau/nvkmd_nouveau_mem.c
@@ -139,6 +139,14 @@ nvkmd_nouveau_alloc_tiled_mem(struct nvkmd_dev *_dev,
    align_B = MAX2(align_B, dev->base.pdev->bind_align_B);
    size_B = align64(size_B, align_B);
 
+   /* NVKMD_MEM_NO_ZERO_INIT IS IGNORED HERE, AND MUST BE. The pages
+    * behind a nouveau BO come from the kernel, which zeroes them
+    * because they may have belonged to another process — a guarantee
+    * about somebody else's data, which no caller of this interface is
+    * in a position to waive. There is also nothing to save: the driver
+    * does not fill them itself, so the flag would turn off a memset
+    * that does not exist. See nvkmd.h.
+    */
    enum nouveau_ws_bo_flags nouveau_flags = domains;
    if (flags & NVKMD_MEM_CAN_MAP)
       nouveau_flags |= NOUVEAU_WS_BO_MAP;
diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nvkmd.h b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nvkmd.h
index ee4bf63..7490c2b 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nvkmd.h
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/nvkmd.h
@@ -58,6 +58,36 @@ enum nvkmd_mem_flags {
 
    /** This memory object has coherent CPU maps */
    NVKMD_MEM_COHERENT   = 1 << 5,
+
+   /** The contents of this memory object need not start out zero.
+    *
+    * A HINT A BACKEND MAY IGNORE, AND A PROMISE THE CALLER MAY NOT.
+    * The caller is asserting that every byte anything will ever read
+    * from this object is written first — by the CPU, by the GPU, or by
+    * the caller's own explicit fill. It is not "I do not mind what is
+    * in there": a consumer that reads a byte nobody wrote gets whatever
+    * the backend's allocator last had there.
+    *
+    * WHY IT EXISTS. On a backend where the kernel hands out fresh
+    * anonymous pages — the nouveau one — this is already true and the
+    * flag costs nothing; that backend ignores it, and MUST, because the
+    * kernel's zeroing is a guarantee about OTHER processes' data and is
+    * not the caller's to waive. On the Horizon backend the allocation
+    * comes from this process's own heap through nvmap, so the zero fill
+    * is a memset this process pays for. MEASURED ON A CONSOLE
+    * 2026-09-07, gpu_memory/alloc: 21 us of a 64 KiB allocation, 182 us
+    * of a 1 MiB one and 1403 us of an 8 MiB one — 1992 us against
+    * 589 us for the same 8 MiB without it.
+    *
+    * NVK ALREADY MODELS THIS, which is what makes the flag safe to
+    * introduce rather than a new promise. nvk_device_memory.c computes
+    * an `enum nvk_memory_init` per allocation and does its own
+    * synchronous fill for NVK_MEMORY_INIT_ZERO — the case
+    * VK_MEMORY_ALLOCATE_ZERO_INITIALIZE_BIT_EXT and NVK_DEBUG=zero_memory
+    * ask for. Everything else is NVK_MEMORY_INIT_NONE, and NONE means
+    * NVK is not relying on the contents.
+    */
+   NVKMD_MEM_NO_ZERO_INIT = 1 << 6,
 };
 
 #define NVKMD_MEM_PLACEMENT_FLAGS \
-- 
